CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ first operand: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ second operand: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ neither: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ failed first operand: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.5\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
N5008 [temp.constr.op]/3: a disjunction is satisfied if either operand is;
[temp.constr.atomic]/3: an atomic constraint whose substitution fails is not
satisfied (not an error).  The front end type-checked a concept's body as a
whole expression, so a substitution failure in the FIRST operand of `||'
made the whole concept-id false and the second operand was never considered
(bin53).  The top-level `&&' and `||' are now evaluated operand by operand
([temp.constr.normal]/1; cpp_typecheckt::convert_concept_initializer).  Root
cause of
cpp20_ranges_basic and cpp20_iterator_t_of_pointer_undeduced_declval failing
with the libstdc++ 16 headers: views::__adaptor::__adaptor_has_simple_extra_args
is `_Adaptor::_S_has_simple_extra_args || _Adaptor::template
_S_has_simple_extra_args<_Args...>', and views::_Take's member is a variable
template, so the first operand fails; _Partial<_Take, int> then used the
primary template (a C++23 explicit object parameter operator()) and
`arr | views::take(3)' was rejected; with GCC 16.2.1 cpp20_ranges_basic now
passes.  Removing the first disjunct from the
preprocessed header makes cpp20_ranges_basic verify.  g++ 13 and clang++ 18
accept this program, and it fails only its WRONG assertion.
